WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
- Before '95, the attention
was around the information.

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:06.330
The internet was actually called

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:08.480
the information superhighway.

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:11.310
It was about looking up stuff,

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:13.530
it was a better encyclopedia,

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:15.940
look at this information.

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:20.350
At the time in 1994, I don't
think anybody walking on earth

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:22.690
understood that the internet would eat up

00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:24.780
almost every aspect of our lives.

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:28.570
Everybody laughed at me
in 1995, when I told them

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:31.420
that I was gonna build
a website to sell wine.

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:34.290
And I'm telling you,
everybody laughed at me,

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:35.940
everybody said, "Nobody's gonna buy..."

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:39.680
I remember being in meetings
where people would say

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:41.670
things like "Gary, this is crazy,

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:44.330
nobody's gonna buy wine on the internet.

00:00:44.330 --> 00:00:45.857
The internet is a fad,"

00:00:46.888 --> 00:00:48.147
And I would reply,

00:00:48.147 --> 00:00:50.660
"Not only are people gonna
buy wine on the internet,

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:53.110
people are gonna buy
everything on the internet."

00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:56.350
- [Gary] You got your perspective.

00:00:57.502 --> 00:00:59.794
(crowd cheers)

00:00:59.794 --> 00:01:01.863
I just wanna be happy,
don't you wanna be happy?

00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:09.169
- As a matter of fact, I
used to use the concept

00:01:09.169 --> 00:01:11.330
of people are gonna buy
tomatoes on the internet

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:13.410
because for some reason,
people are very passionate

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:15.950
about touching tomatoes at the supermarket

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:17.440
and that very much upset people.

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:21.010
And I remember when Fresh
Direct came to New York

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:23.960
and people were buying
tomatoes on the internet

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:25.090
it was a very big deal for me

00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:27.050
'cause I emailed a lot
of old friends and said,

00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:28.203
I told you so.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:32.007
I think that the blockchain

00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:36.420
in the consumer way that we're
talking about it with NFTs

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:39.520
is the biggest thing that's
happened since 1994/95.

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:42.680
And I think everybody here
should spend as many hours

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:45.280
educating themselves on
what I'm really saying.

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:47.810
It goes far beyond board A or B friends.

00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:50.370
It is a monumental shift.

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:53.480
And right now we're
looking at collectability

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:55.610
with a hint of utility.

00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:58.900
I think very quickly, we're
gonna get into a scaled world

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:02.530
of utility with hints of
collectibility on the back end.

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:05.510
And when happens, it's
gonna start really moving.

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:07.750
- What do you tell somebody who says,

00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:09.550
I don't really know about NFTs.

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:11.730
I don't know what that whole
world's about is it, are they--

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:14.050
- Meaning the people
that say, I don't know

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:15.600
or the people that are saying it's a scam,

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:17.410
it's full of shit, it's a fad.

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:19.490
- I think the people who really
just haven't taken the time

00:02:19.490 --> 00:02:22.880
to look into it or you know,
given it any thought at all,

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:25.050
even if you don't invest or are involved,

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:27.140
but know what it's about.

00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:31.223
- I don't really have a lot of passion

00:02:31.223 --> 00:02:33.273
of really saying anything to them,

00:02:35.055 --> 00:02:40.055
meaning I'm not in any need
of everybody understanding it.

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:43.420
It's gonna happen anyway.

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:45.180
What I would say to people I care about

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:47.920
is it's a missed opportunity

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:50.493
for you not to get educated on this,

00:02:51.596 --> 00:02:54.650
but I often tell friends and family

00:02:54.650 --> 00:02:57.290
and people I care about in my community

00:02:57.290 --> 00:02:59.490
a very simple truth in moments like this.

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:02.720
I have no interest in convincing anybody

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:06.140
on earth that this is
important or opportunistic.

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:10.000
I have conviction in my belief that it is

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:12.240
but I'm not in the convincing business.

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:15.400
I'm in the business of
putting things on the record

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:17.103
and being historically correct.

00:03:18.839 --> 00:03:20.279
- All right, so.

00:03:20.279 --> 00:03:23.279
(audience applauds)

00:03:24.597 --> 00:03:26.960
- And this is an interesting point

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:31.710
and also being prepared to have
to deploy a lot of humility

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:34.720
if I'm historically incorrect, you know.

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:36.910
For me the reason I'm
comfortable doing this is

00:03:36.910 --> 00:03:39.660
if God forbid 'cause I
don't want it to happen,

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:44.660
this whole thing isn't real
and the blockchain disappears.

00:03:45.370 --> 00:03:49.220
I'm mentally prepared for saying
here's why I didn't see it.

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:51.690
This happened, and this
happened and this happened.

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:53.070
And I think that's a very important part

00:03:53.070 --> 00:03:54.120
of many people's journeys.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:57.670
I think a lot of you here
don't produce content

00:03:57.670 --> 00:04:00.860
or don't do things out
of fear of being wrong.

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:05.030
And I wanna remind you that being wrong

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:06.930
is not only okay

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:09.170
it's actually a preference.

00:04:09.170 --> 00:04:10.760
It means you're pushing.

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:12.710
What's not okay, is when you're wrong,

00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:14.510
not being accountable and owning it.

00:04:15.982 --> 00:04:19.547
(audience applauds)

00:04:19.547 --> 00:04:21.540
- Now obviously today we're at Coders HQ.

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:23.840
How can coders or the coding community,

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:26.620
or even like digital artists jump onto

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:27.920
the blockchain of Web 3.0.

00:04:29.718 --> 00:04:31.960
- You know, it's funny 'cause a lot of you

00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:33.760
probably are aware of my content.

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:37.200
I often make fun of school or college

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:40.200
but what's unfortunate is that
people aren't really hearing

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:41.033
what I'm saying.

00:04:41.033 --> 00:04:43.190
I believe that education
is the most important thing

00:04:43.190 --> 00:04:44.460
in the world.

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:46.040
I think the way education

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:49.640
is currently being sold in
the world has lost its way

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:51.550
in the reality of our world.

00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:52.530
The answer to your question

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:54.260
is how do you take advantage of it?

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:56.860
Education, in December, 2020

00:04:56.860 --> 00:04:59.410
when I sensed through my strength,

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:01.480
which is consumer intuition,

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:04.140
that the NFT thing was
finally getting close,

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:07.800
I spent hundreds of
hours educating myself.

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:12.470
It started with very simple
videos of what is an NFT today.

00:05:12.470 --> 00:05:15.350
'Cause I remembered what
I thought it was in 2017

00:05:15.350 --> 00:05:17.070
when I first saw CryptoKitties.

00:05:17.070 --> 00:05:18.760
But I knew that I hadn't
been paying attention.

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:20.363
So I had to reeducate myself.

00:05:21.790 --> 00:05:23.130
The answer is education.

00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:26.560
Like every Web 2.0 developer in the world

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:29.230
should be learning how
to develop for Web 3.0

00:05:29.230 --> 00:05:31.800
because it's lucratively
exciting for them.

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:34.890
There's incredible demand
for Web 3.0 developers.

00:05:34.890 --> 00:05:37.070
And really it's not a very big leap

00:05:37.070 --> 00:05:39.080
for a Web 2.0 developer to get to Web 3.0.

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:41.560
There's just certain things
you have to know and understand

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:44.040
that, you know a decentralized server

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:46.130
is a very different environment
than owning your servers.

00:05:46.130 --> 00:05:49.000
Which is why so many
launches last year struggled

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:51.870
because a lot of
developers didn't remember

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:53.730
that they were really
building on the blockchain.

00:05:53.730 --> 00:05:56.470
They were so accustomed to
building on their own server.

00:05:56.470 --> 00:05:57.490
They got caught.

00:05:57.490 --> 00:05:59.193
You could see the traits.

00:06:00.454 --> 00:06:02.080
So the answer to your
question is just education.

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:05.043
Like put in the time and
effort to be educated.

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:08.090
And another little side note,

00:06:08.090 --> 00:06:10.460
a lot of the energy right now in Web 3.0

00:06:10.460 --> 00:06:13.120
is unfortunately built around greed

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:15.360
and short term financial behavior

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:17.340
which is a very different game

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:18.900
than Web 1.0 and 2.0 were built on

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:22.330
because there wasn't this much
money being thrown around.

00:06:22.330 --> 00:06:24.740
So if you're in this room just make sure

00:06:24.740 --> 00:06:27.790
that you're not getting clouded by greed.

00:06:27.790 --> 00:06:31.350
Which what that really means
is once you have a hypothesis

00:06:31.350 --> 00:06:33.910
make sure that your
hypothesis is not grounded

00:06:33.910 --> 00:06:35.740
in what you'd like to happen for you.

00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:37.840
Make sure you're double
checking everything

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:40.430
in a more holistic point of view on it.

00:06:40.430 --> 00:06:43.030
- Can you give any advice to
coders who wanna capitalize

00:06:43.030 --> 00:06:45.130
on their code and coding capability?

00:06:45.130 --> 00:06:46.950
Has there been any like drive

00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:50.480
towards blockchain Web 3.0
for coders in specific?

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:52.480
I mean we know about the NFTs and--

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:55.760
- Well, I mean, you
know, any viable project

00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:57.880
that's launching on its own website

00:06:57.880 --> 00:07:00.193
and is minting a project
needs development.

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:04.020
You know, so, you know,
I have a lot of advice.

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:05.850
It's called understand how to code

00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:08.480
on decentralized servers, right?

00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:12.890
It's like get accustomed
to the new behaviors.

00:07:12.890 --> 00:07:14.468
There's incredible, I mean,

00:07:14.468 --> 00:07:18.160
this is one of the great
eras for developers.

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:21.280
There's so much security
that needs to be figured out.

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:23.820
There's so much bridging
that needs to be figured out.

00:07:23.820 --> 00:07:26.590
There's so much capabilities of minting

00:07:26.590 --> 00:07:28.620
that we haven't even explored yet.

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:30.920
I mean, this is probably
the most exciting time

00:07:30.920 --> 00:07:34.650
for a developer since
probably 2005, 6, 7, 8

00:07:34.650 --> 00:07:37.433
when the Web 2.0 movement was happening.

00:07:39.441 --> 00:07:42.100
So, you know, it's funny
for me to even think

00:07:43.072 --> 00:07:47.010
that developers don't see how
big of an opportunity this is.

00:07:47.010 --> 00:07:50.030
I think it goes back to
education and execution.

00:07:50.030 --> 00:07:51.180
- Yeah. I think, you know

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:52.187
you touched on it a little bit as well.

00:07:52.187 --> 00:07:54.560
One of the other questions
was about how initiatives

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:57.550
like Coders HQ are focused on enabling

00:07:57.550 --> 00:07:58.630
the coding communities.

00:07:58.630 --> 00:08:01.348
And you know, you said
education obviously,

00:08:01.348 --> 00:08:03.570
but you know how could they
focus on driving communities

00:08:03.570 --> 00:08:07.377
towards you know, so
what kind of education,

00:08:07.377 --> 00:08:11.830
how do they shift from how
it currently is to Web 3.0.

00:08:12.760 --> 00:08:13.920
Where does it start?

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:18.320
- It starts with believing
that the opportunity exists.

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:20.920
You're not gonna spend 50 hours learning

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:22.620
if you don't think it's there.

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:26.640
So, you know, I remember when
Ruby on Rails as a language

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:29.700
started catch attention
back in 04, 5, 6, 7.

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:32.010
I'm trying to remember exactly the timing.

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:33.850
You had to believe that
that was a language

00:08:33.850 --> 00:08:36.870
that was gonna catch you know, heat.

00:08:36.870 --> 00:08:39.640
Otherwise you weren't
gonna stop coding in PHP.

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:41.740
And then of course it didn't catch

00:08:41.740 --> 00:08:43.420
as long term and it evolves

00:08:43.420 --> 00:08:45.360
and that's just the game
of development, right?

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:49.270
Some things stick some things
don't but you learn, you know

00:08:49.270 --> 00:08:50.670
you learn from certain things

00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:53.610
in learning that language might
have helped you be stronger

00:08:53.610 --> 00:08:55.220
at the next thing.

00:08:55.220 --> 00:08:58.240
I think it starts with conviction around

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:00.420
the movement is actually happening.

00:09:00.420 --> 00:09:01.963
And again,

00:09:03.080 --> 00:09:04.210
I think the biggest reason,

00:09:04.210 --> 00:09:07.170
so many of your friends
don't see the NFT thing

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:08.640
or the blockchain thing

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:10.810
is 'cause they're bringing
their internet perspective

00:09:10.810 --> 00:09:12.390
to the blockchain.

00:09:12.390 --> 00:09:14.130
People make jokes like that's so stupid.

00:09:14.130 --> 00:09:15.240
I'm gonna right click and save.

00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:17.370
That's an internet lingo.

00:09:17.370 --> 00:09:18.690
That's what we did on the internet.

00:09:18.690 --> 00:09:20.410
That's not how the blockchain works.

00:09:20.410 --> 00:09:22.080
When people are like, this is stupid.

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:25.690
Like who owns, like people
say the most basic things.

00:09:25.690 --> 00:09:28.120
Like it is much easier for me to know

00:09:28.120 --> 00:09:31.130
if you own something on the
blockchain than for me to know

00:09:31.130 --> 00:09:32.960
if you own something in real life.

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:34.370
We can all go outside right now

00:09:34.370 --> 00:09:36.400
and take a picture next to a car,

00:09:36.400 --> 00:09:39.120
put it on social media and
make pretend it's our car.

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:40.530
People do that.

00:09:40.530 --> 00:09:42.870
You can't do that with an NFT.

00:09:42.870 --> 00:09:46.370
I can look it up in three
seconds and see you don't own it.

00:09:46.370 --> 00:09:51.370
So what's really fascinating
is, is the concept of

00:09:51.450 --> 00:09:53.950
are we grasping the opportunity

00:09:53.950 --> 00:09:56.930
and are we learning how to not bring

00:09:56.930 --> 00:10:00.050
our Web 2.0 development brain to Web 3.0.

00:10:00.050 --> 00:10:04.110
Which is why so many
projects struggled last year.

00:10:04.110 --> 00:10:08.430
Incredible developers didn't even realize

00:10:08.430 --> 00:10:11.180
that subconsciously they
didn't understand the pitfalls

00:10:11.180 --> 00:10:13.690
they were gonna have at mint
because they didn't understand

00:10:13.690 --> 00:10:15.100
that they didn't control the server.

00:10:15.100 --> 00:10:16.420
Even though it's decentralized,

00:10:16.420 --> 00:10:18.570
it's just fascinating to watch.

00:10:18.570 --> 00:10:21.210
So I think A, recognizing the situation

00:10:21.210 --> 00:10:24.760
and then B having the humility
to really put in the work

00:10:24.760 --> 00:10:25.783
to do it properly.

00:10:27.610 --> 00:10:29.550
- What are the factors
that drive your decision

00:10:29.550 --> 00:10:32.160
into investing into digital companies?

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:34.560
I mean, you did it, you
know, at a very early stage

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:35.930
like you said, with the wine company

00:10:35.930 --> 00:10:37.730
and also invest into tech companies.

00:10:38.896 --> 00:10:42.820
And now you're very ahead
in the Web 3.0 world.

00:10:42.820 --> 00:10:44.140
- My--
- Blockchain.

00:10:44.140 --> 00:10:46.620
- My greatest strength is intuition

00:10:46.620 --> 00:10:48.010
around what people are gonna do

00:10:48.010 --> 00:10:50.010
before they realize they're gonna do it.

00:10:50.980 --> 00:10:53.540
That has been my real business gift

00:10:53.540 --> 00:10:57.280
that I'm able to somehow

00:10:57.280 --> 00:10:59.470
intuitively understand

00:10:59.470 --> 00:11:01.070
that you are going to do this

00:11:01.070 --> 00:11:03.230
even though you say you're not going to.

00:11:03.230 --> 00:11:06.070
And I think a lot of that has to do with

00:11:06.070 --> 00:11:07.760
a couple of core beliefs I have.

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:09.690
Which is A,

00:11:09.690 --> 00:11:12.270
I don't think humans understand

00:11:12.270 --> 00:11:15.640
how much convenience will
always win in the end.

00:11:15.640 --> 00:11:20.640
That you know that I remember
a Blackberry to iPhone debate.

00:11:20.760 --> 00:11:23.030
So many people never thought
they would get an iPhone

00:11:23.030 --> 00:11:25.950
'cause they liked the
buttons on the Blackberry.

00:11:25.950 --> 00:11:28.280
But I remember just thinking
every day from day one,

00:11:28.280 --> 00:11:30.760
I'm like, but it has the internet on it.

00:11:30.760 --> 00:11:34.510
And the internet is more
powerful than touching buttons.

00:11:34.510 --> 00:11:36.570
The internet will beat the buttons.

00:11:36.570 --> 00:11:38.250
And I think that's how I think about

00:11:38.250 --> 00:11:39.530
the consumer blockchain.

00:11:39.530 --> 00:11:44.530
I already understand that
NFTs representing the mundane

00:11:44.850 --> 00:11:46.740
like a receipt or a ticket

00:11:46.740 --> 00:11:49.860
or a bracelet is gonna
be better for people.

00:11:49.860 --> 00:11:51.900
They just don't see it yet.

00:11:51.900 --> 00:11:55.080
Like, and so, you know, for me

00:11:56.010 --> 00:11:59.430
what I do is first I
understand the consumer truth.

00:11:59.430 --> 00:12:01.450
People are gonna do this.

00:12:01.450 --> 00:12:02.870
That's intuition.

00:12:02.870 --> 00:12:06.470
Pattern recognition, my
obsession with history.

00:12:06.470 --> 00:12:09.320
There's a lot of things
that help me be good at it.

00:12:09.320 --> 00:12:11.480
And then the next thing I do after that is

00:12:12.481 --> 00:12:16.010
I invest based on the human
being that's running it.

00:12:16.010 --> 00:12:19.080
I think the reason that I'll
be successful in NFT investing

00:12:19.080 --> 00:12:20.700
and a lot of people are gonna lose

00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:23.880
is I don't believe in what a
lot of people accept as truth.

00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:26.874
For example, so many people talk,

00:12:26.874 --> 00:12:29.040
Gary, this NFT has,

00:12:29.040 --> 00:12:30.210
I think a lot of you,

00:12:30.210 --> 00:12:33.800
if you are in this space
remember the last several months,

00:12:33.800 --> 00:12:36.000
I love the roadmap.

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:37.100
That's why I'm investing.

00:12:37.100 --> 00:12:39.645
And I was like, but
that's like a pitch deck.

00:12:39.645 --> 00:12:40.540
(audience laughs)

00:12:40.540 --> 00:12:42.410
Just 'cause you say
you're gonna do something,

00:12:42.410 --> 00:12:44.420
doesn't mean that's a
good reason to invest.

00:12:44.420 --> 00:12:47.090
I learned that lesson in my career.

00:12:47.090 --> 00:12:48.530
That was a good deck

00:12:48.530 --> 00:12:50.700
but they didn't know how
to actually run a company.

00:12:50.700 --> 00:12:52.470
Number two, Gary, you don't get it.

00:12:52.470 --> 00:12:54.930
This is gonna win
because of the community.

00:12:54.930 --> 00:12:56.290
Well, the community's only as good

00:12:56.290 --> 00:12:58.270
as the people executing the project.

00:12:58.270 --> 00:13:00.600
These communities are not really built,

00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:02.130
the far majority on communities,

00:13:02.130 --> 00:13:03.710
they're built on the fact
you're rooting for it

00:13:03.710 --> 00:13:06.160
'cause you bought it and
you want it to go up.

00:13:06.160 --> 00:13:08.110
You're financially incentivized.

00:13:08.110 --> 00:13:10.540
Just like people that bought Beanie Babies

00:13:10.540 --> 00:13:14.190
cared about it to just flip,
thus Beanie Babies died

00:13:14.190 --> 00:13:16.610
because they're not Disney,
they're not transformers,

00:13:16.610 --> 00:13:17.710
they're not Marvel,

00:13:17.710 --> 00:13:19.660
where you actually care
about the characters.

00:13:19.660 --> 00:13:21.950
You just bought the Beanie
Baby to sell the Beanie Baby

00:13:21.950 --> 00:13:24.290
which is 99% of the behavior in NFTs.

00:13:24.290 --> 00:13:26.540
Which is why I put out
content saying 99% of these

00:13:26.540 --> 00:13:29.980
are gonna fail because
I know human behavior.

00:13:29.980 --> 00:13:33.840
For me, what I do is believe
in a human truth, a thesis.

00:13:33.840 --> 00:13:35.820
And then I start looking
for the people that I think

00:13:35.820 --> 00:13:39.810
are capable of driving that
to a successful outcome,

00:13:39.810 --> 00:13:41.540
which is something I don't think

00:13:41.540 --> 00:13:43.890
people are spending enough
time on in NFT land.

00:13:43.890 --> 00:13:44.723
I mean there are people

00:13:44.723 --> 00:13:47.530
spending hundreds of thousands
of dollars on projects

00:13:47.530 --> 00:13:50.640
where the founders are anonymous.

00:13:50.640 --> 00:13:52.300
The reason there's so many rug pulls

00:13:52.300 --> 00:13:54.020
is 'cause people are making bad decisions

00:13:54.020 --> 00:13:55.410
because they're in a gold rush

00:13:55.410 --> 00:13:58.290
and everybody's so over focused

00:13:58.290 --> 00:14:02.173
on making money in 24 hours
that they're gonna lose money.

00:14:03.800 --> 00:14:06.950
- You always seem to be
one step ahead of the game.

00:14:06.950 --> 00:14:09.700
The game right now is 3.0 and blockchain.

00:14:09.700 --> 00:14:12.580
Is there anything else
that you're thinking about

00:14:13.719 --> 00:14:17.210
or is on your plate that it's coming up?

00:14:17.210 --> 00:14:21.483
- No, I don't allow
myself to think that way.

00:14:23.040 --> 00:14:26.300
I have no emotion that
tomorrow needs to be

00:14:26.300 --> 00:14:27.960
a new playing field.

00:14:27.960 --> 00:14:31.410
What I do is I spend all my time absorbing

00:14:31.410 --> 00:14:34.550
what humans are doing so that I'm ready

00:14:34.550 --> 00:14:36.530
when something has emerged.

00:14:36.530 --> 00:14:40.320
I didn't force NFTs into the lexicon.

00:14:40.320 --> 00:14:43.490
I watched people and realized NFTs were

00:14:43.490 --> 00:14:45.163
on the precipice of happening.

00:14:46.270 --> 00:14:50.053
So for me I'm just paying attention,

00:14:51.230 --> 00:14:53.140
but I only get interested when things are

00:14:53.140 --> 00:14:55.440
on the precipice of scale.

00:14:55.440 --> 00:14:57.630
My career, when it's looked on

00:14:57.630 --> 00:14:59.130
when I'm long gone

00:14:59.130 --> 00:15:01.850
will probably be much
more of a story of timing

00:15:01.850 --> 00:15:03.410
than anything else.

00:15:03.410 --> 00:15:06.780
I bought Ethereum in 2015.

00:15:06.780 --> 00:15:10.840
And you can find clips of me
talking about it in 2017, 2018

00:15:10.840 --> 00:15:12.690
but they're very far and few between.

00:15:12.690 --> 00:15:15.830
I bought it, I understood the concept

00:15:15.830 --> 00:15:18.000
I watched from afar.

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:21.410
And then when I felt
that the summer of 2020

00:15:21.410 --> 00:15:25.980
was showing me indications
because of Top Shot and Flow,

00:15:25.980 --> 00:15:30.220
because of Punks that
something was brewing.

00:15:30.220 --> 00:15:33.610
Then I committed to the
time in the winter to say,

00:15:33.610 --> 00:15:34.670
is it ready?

00:15:34.670 --> 00:15:37.970
And then by early January, February 2021,

00:15:37.970 --> 00:15:39.820
I'm like, oh my God, it's ready.

00:15:39.820 --> 00:15:41.570
Then I got very loud.

00:15:41.570 --> 00:15:42.690
You know, it's funny.

00:15:42.690 --> 00:15:46.130
I'm so frantic, high energy loud.

00:15:46.130 --> 00:15:48.680
I understand my communication style,

00:15:48.680 --> 00:15:51.010
which I think confuses people at times

00:15:51.010 --> 00:15:52.860
to think I might be a little bit

00:15:52.860 --> 00:15:54.860
throw against the wall
and see what sticks.

00:15:54.860 --> 00:15:56.580
A lot of people say, you know

00:15:56.580 --> 00:15:58.310
when someone says something nice about me,

00:15:58.310 --> 00:16:00.110
like, man, you've been
really right about stuff.

00:16:00.110 --> 00:16:01.320
I'll always see a lot of comments.

00:16:01.320 --> 00:16:03.640
I'm like, yeah, if you predict 900 things

00:16:03.640 --> 00:16:05.792
three are gonna be right.

00:16:05.792 --> 00:16:09.010
And I always reply to those people.

00:16:09.010 --> 00:16:12.100
I'm like, hey, would
love to have you show me

00:16:12.100 --> 00:16:14.143
the video of what I predicted wrong?

00:16:15.080 --> 00:16:19.890
I think what I do very well
seems very counter to my energy,

00:16:19.890 --> 00:16:21.210
which is the following.

00:16:21.210 --> 00:16:23.360
There's a great saying
that a lot of people

00:16:23.360 --> 00:16:27.363
really believe in, which
is measure twice cut once.

00:16:28.530 --> 00:16:32.890
I believe that I measure
50 times and cut once.

00:16:32.890 --> 00:16:35.490
It's just that after I'm
done measuring 50 times

00:16:35.490 --> 00:16:37.743
and I'm ready, I cut loudest.

00:16:39.200 --> 00:16:41.850
I also feel that because of my intuition

00:16:41.850 --> 00:16:44.480
and because of my listening strengths

00:16:44.480 --> 00:16:47.700
that I do see things a hair early.

00:16:47.700 --> 00:16:50.750
I, once I sense it and I taste it,

00:16:50.750 --> 00:16:54.140
I'm so convicted that it's a
feeling that I've known before

00:16:54.140 --> 00:16:56.620
that I'll put in the work so fast,

00:16:56.620 --> 00:16:59.400
which allows me to speak to it earlier.

00:16:59.400 --> 00:17:02.180
I was right about TikTok, right?

00:17:02.180 --> 00:17:03.960
I was right about social.

00:17:03.960 --> 00:17:08.679
I was right about E-commerce
and content and many platforms.

00:17:08.679 --> 00:17:11.469
And it's because I put in the work

00:17:11.469 --> 00:17:14.810
and I don't fear being wrong.

00:17:14.810 --> 00:17:16.330
But I'll be very honest with you.

00:17:16.330 --> 00:17:18.160
The only reason there's so many people

00:17:18.160 --> 00:17:22.543
in this room right now is
'cause I've been right, right?

00:17:23.405 --> 00:17:26.343
Like, and so--
(audience applauds)

00:17:29.500 --> 00:17:30.850
I tell, you know, a
lot of people are like,

00:17:30.850 --> 00:17:33.540
well Gary, your, of course
your NFT project's doing well.

00:17:33.540 --> 00:17:34.760
You have a huge community.

00:17:34.760 --> 00:17:38.999
I'm like one that I
earned, like all, like--

00:17:38.999 --> 00:17:41.070
(audience applauds)

00:17:41.070 --> 00:17:43.330
I'm fascinated by that.

00:17:43.330 --> 00:17:45.760
And so I'm incredibly thoughtful

00:17:45.760 --> 00:17:47.240
of what comes out of my mouth.

00:17:47.240 --> 00:17:49.150
I think a lot of you that have
followed me for a long time.

00:17:49.150 --> 00:17:52.240
I don't say that many
things, I'm pretty narrow.

00:17:52.240 --> 00:17:55.883
I have a good knack of saying
them 8,000 different ways.

00:17:56.760 --> 00:17:58.440
I may say them in new platforms

00:17:58.440 --> 00:18:00.550
in different content formats.

00:18:00.550 --> 00:18:02.820
But you know, to me,

00:18:02.820 --> 00:18:05.130
I'm a 100% sure

00:18:05.130 --> 00:18:07.500
that the consumer
blockchain is gonna matter

00:18:07.500 --> 00:18:10.570
and I'm trying to learn
and build community.

00:18:10.570 --> 00:18:12.150
And more importantly,

00:18:12.150 --> 00:18:16.750
it is incredibly intoxicating
to have your community benefit

00:18:16.750 --> 00:18:18.460
from your observations.

00:18:18.460 --> 00:18:21.640
And so that has been a beautiful part

00:18:21.640 --> 00:18:22.833
of this journey for me.

00:18:23.835 --> 00:18:26.418
(upbeat music)

